      overview: "The author explains the higher objectives (Maqasid) that Shari'ah rules are designed to serve, distinguishing primary objectives (protection of religion, life, progeny, property, intellect, honour) from secondary ones (justice, social security, peace, cooperation, moral values).",
      simpleExplanation: "Shari'ah isn't just a list of rules — it exists to protect six fundamental things: religion, life, family, property, the mind, and honour/dignity. From these flow further goals like justice, helping the needy, peace, and promoting good over evil. The overall aim is human well-being and happiness, in this life and the next — not just material comfort.",
      academicExplanation: "Studying Maqasid matters because they reflect the spirit of Shari'ah and guide jurists' Ijtihad/Qiyas in determining permissibility. Catering to human well-being in this world and the Hereafter, or relieving hardship, is the basic objective; Islam takes a positive view of life (man as vicegerent/Khalifah of God), requiring a morally responsible life, fair earning, and treating wealth as a trust (Amanah) for which account is rendered to Allah. Unlike conventional economics (where livelihood/economic development is the ultimate goal), Islamic economics treats livelihood as necessary but not the sole purpose of life — the Hereafter is the real concern. Jurists (Ghazali, Shatbi, later Tahir ibn Ashoor) identified six Primary Objectives via inductive survey of Qur'an/Sunnah: protection/preservation of (1) Religion, (2) Life (including the law of Qisas and provision of basic necessities), (3) Progeny/family unit (marriage, chastity, upbringing), (4) Property (sanctity of Halal wealth, discouraging wealth concentration, governed by the law of Mu'amalat), (5) Intellect (acquiring knowledge), (6) Honour (prohibition of false accusation, right to privacy). These lead to five Secondary Objectives: establishing justice/equity, promoting social security/mutual help (especially for the poor), maintaining peace/security, promoting cooperation in good and prohibiting evil, and promoting universal moral values/preservation of nature. Chapra is quoted arguing well-being (in the Islamic sense) must include humanitarian/spiritual goals — social equality, need fulfilment, full employment, equitable distribution, economic stability without heavy debt-servicing burdens, plus family/social solidarity — and that material and spiritual well-being are interrelated, not independent. The author also lists eight basic socio-economic rights derived from Qur'an/Sunnah study: to safety, to be informed, to choose, to be heard, to satisfaction of basic needs, to redress, to education, and to a healthy environment; rulers/regulators must protect these against unscrupulous exploitation.",
      examExplanation: "Six Primary Objectives (Maqasid) of Shari'ah: protection of Religion, Life, Progeny, Property, Intellect, Honour. Five Secondary Objectives flowing from these: justice/equity, social security & mutual help, peace & security, promotion of good/prohibition of evil, and universal moral values. Chapra's expanded view of 'well-being' includes both material (need fulfilment, equitable distribution, stability without heavy debt burdens) and spiritual/humanitarian goals (family and social solidarity) — the two are interrelated, not separable. Eight derived socio-economic rights: safety, information, choice, being heard, basic-needs satisfaction, redress, education, healthy environment.",
