      title: "Why Study Islamic Economics? The Integrated Islamic Worldview",
      overview: "The author argues economics deserves serious study within Islam because Islam does not separate 'worldly' from 'religious' life, and because the failure of secular economic systems to solve global poverty and injustice makes an alternative worth exploring.",
      simpleExplanation: "Islam doesn't separate spiritual life from economic life — earning a living and spending it wisely are themselves acts of devotion. Since conventional 'trickle-down' economics and the shrinking welfare state have failed to fix poverty and inequality, studying an economics grounded in justice and care for the poor is a strategic necessity, not just an academic exercise.",
      academicExplanation: "The author argues economic sustenance and religious belief are the two central concerns of human life, so Islam integrates devotion with guidance on economy, politics and international relations (Islam rejects a 'pious person' vs 'worldly person' dichotomy), citing Qur'an 2:177 on righteousness including both belief and spending on kin, orphans, the needy, travellers, etc., interpreted by Anis Ahmad as reflecting the Qur'an's social/human dimensions and its relevance to all humanity, not just Muslims. M.A. Mannan's seven imperatives for studying Islamic economics — ideological, economic, social, ethical, political, historical, international — are cited, along with his view that Islamic economics (unlike secular economics) cannot remain neutral between ends and must engage social, political, ethical and moral issues. Beyond intra-Muslim underdevelopment, the author cites the broader global failure of secularist systems: trickle-down theory and related theories of automatic poverty alleviation via growth have failed; the Western welfare state has given way to 'the least government is the best government'; rising 'hyper-consumption' among the rich (both developed and developing world) coexists with mass poverty and illiteracy. He also traces this partly to the Enlightenment's secularist worldview, which treated revealed religious truths as mere inventions, weakening religion's collective moral-sanctioning role. Since Islamic economics centers on care for the poor and socio-economic justice, studying it is framed as strategically necessary for economists and policymakers, grounded in the Qur'an's broad values (an owner's attitude to property, society's duty to the needy, cooperative economic relationships, and bias against wealth concentration).",
      examExplanation: "The author gives two main rationales for studying Islamic economics: (1) Islam integrates religious and economic/worldly life (no pious-vs-worldly dichotomy — cf. Qur'an 2:177); (2) the demonstrated failure of secular economic systems (trickle-down theory, shrinking welfare states, Enlightenment-driven secularism) to resolve poverty/inequality, alongside rising 'hyper-consumption' amid mass poverty, makes an alternative rooted in justice and care for the poor strategically relevant, not merely intra-Muslim.",
