      id: "ch2-t4",
      sectionNumber: "2.3.1",
      sectionTitle: "The Role of Islamic Economists",
      title: "Historical Contributions of Classical and Modern Islamic Economists",
      overview: "The author surveys classical Muslim jurist-economists (especially Ibn Khaldun) and traces the modern revival of Islamic economics through named scholars, noting that this body of work remains fragmented rather than a unified model.",
      simpleExplanation: "Long before modern economics existed, Muslim scholars like Ibn Khaldun were already analyzing markets, taxation, labor and the State's economic role — Ibn Khaldun is credited with anticipating ideas later attributed to Adam Smith, Malthus and Keynes. In modern times, many economists have revived and modernized this thinking, but their work remains scattered across separate topics rather than combined into one complete economic model.",
      academicExplanation: "Early Islamic jurists advised rulers on economic behaviour/policy and later analysed trading, prices, money, profit-sharing, taxes and development, emphasizing ethics, justice, need fulfilment, efficiency, freedom, growth and development. Named contributors include the Imams of the juristic schools (Abu Hanifa, Malik, Shafii, Ahmad ibn-e-Hanbal) and others (al-Sheibani, Abu Yusuf, al-Mawardi, Nizamul Mulk Tusi, Nasiruddin Tusi, Ghazali, Ibn Taymiyah, Ibn Qayyim, Ibn Khaldun, al-Maqrizi, Shah Waliullah). Ibn Khaldun (1332-1406) receives the most detailed treatment: his Muqaddamah (praised by Arnold Toynbee as unmatched in its kind) supported free private enterprise, opposed government interference in markets via inequitable taxation or direct production/distribution participation, and warned that State monopoly ruins the economy. He is credited with anticipating Adam Smith (labour value theory), Malthus (population theory) and Keynes (normative State role), plus a coherent theory of economic growth — making him arguably the founder of economic science; he also linked productivity to market extent, division of labour and specialization, and warned that the growth of absolute State power (funded by rising taxation/confiscation and expanding bureaucracy/army/police) causes economic decline. Ibn Taymiyah discussed Thaman-e-mithl (normal/market price or wage), economic freedom, market pricing, the ombudsman's role, and government's functions. Nasiruddin Tusi's 13th-century Memorandum guided Mongol-era Iranian financial administration; Shah Waliullah discussed production/exchange principles. Maurice Lombard's The Golden Age of Islam is quoted describing the Muslim world's medieval role as the 'driving force' of trade/culture (unified by the Dinar and Arabic as commercial language) while the West was a relative 'void', its later 11th-century decline into regional fragmentation, and its continued influence in science/medicine/philosophy even post-decline. The modern revival is traced through first-generation pioneers (Syed Qutab, Mawdudi, Baqar Sadre, Hameedullah, etc.) and subsequent economists (Anwar Iqbal Qureshi, Nejatullah Siddiqi, M.A. Mannan, M. Umer Chapra, Abbas Mirakhor, Mohsin S. Khan, and many others) who have addressed most areas of modern economics (market forces, production, distribution, money, self-interest, welfare economics, government finance). However, the author stresses this remains segmented work — not yet integrated into one comprehensive model — with inconsistent implementation across countries, partly because value-judgement-laden areas resist quantification/uniformity.",
      examExplanation: "Ibn Khaldun (1332-1406), author of the Muqaddamah, is the standout classical figure: he supported free private enterprise, opposed State market interference and monopoly, and is credited with anticipating Adam Smith (labour value), Malthus (population) and Keynes (State's normative role) — arguably making him the founder of economic science. Ibn Taymiyah contributed concepts like Thaman-e-mithl (fair/market price). The modern revival (20th century onward) produced extensive segmental work (Siddiqi, Chapra, Mannan, Mirakhor, Mohsin Khan, etc.) across banking, Zakat, fiscal policy and regulation, but the author stresses this has not yet been unified into one comprehensive Islamic economic model, and implementation across countries lacks uniformity.",
