      overview: "The author distinguishes positive economics (value-neutral, descriptive) from normative/welfare economics (value-laden, prescriptive), and situates Islamic economics as normative but grounded in market mechanisms, moderated by ethics and limited State intervention (Al-Hisbah).",
      simpleExplanation: "Positive economics just describes how people actually behave, without judging right or wrong. Normative economics makes recommendations based on values. Islamic economics is normative — it wants outcomes shaped by justice and welfare — but it still uses markets and generally lets prices move freely, stepping in (through an ombudsman-like institution called Hisbah) only when monopolistic or unjust practices distort the market.",
      academicExplanation: "Positive economics (per Lionel Robbins's definition) is value-neutral, dealing scientifically with behaviour under scarcity — describing, not prescribing. Normative/welfare economics ('political economy') necessarily involves value judgements. The Islamic approach treats wealth creation as a means to satisfy human needs and support society, not for boasting or oppression (Qur'an 28:77 on seeking the Hereafter without neglecting one's worldly portion), requiring believers to weigh their economic activities' impact on others, with the State controlling harmful wants via a 'filtering process' and restructuring resource transfers to realize dual (worldly/Hereafter) objectives. Unlike conventional welfare economics' utilitarian 'greatest satisfaction of the greatest number', Islamic economics aims chiefly at removing injustice/inequality while still accepting core market-economy concomitants (ownership rights, freedom of enterprise, competition) — differing from present market systems in vision. Shari'ah 'indicates directions' toward justice/well-being/security/knowledge without coercively imposing them, aiming for equitable (not equal) distribution — likened to blood distributed unequally among body organs according to need/function. Because Shari'ah is noncoercive, the market is treated as a natural mechanism of ethical transformation: the Prophet (pbuh) discouraged price-fixing intervention during ordinary market-driven price fluctuations, but where undue monopolistic/unjust pricing or distribution existed, Al-Hisbah (the institution of the ombudsman) was empowered as a social regulatory body to restore just market functioning.",
